\xspace
\section*{How to Install}
\xsp
{\bf Step 1 }
\xsp
Change  a directory which you want to install and type in 
          {\sf tar xvf ... }. 
\xsp
          It creates the following directories 
\xsp
\begin{indention}{12mm}%%
{\sf 
                source \\
                root \\
                seed  \\
                include \\
                fplincl \\
                output \\
	        samples\\
	        bin\\
	        doc\\
}
\end{indention}%%
           and file {\sf create.ibm} in the main directory.
\xspace
{\bf Step 2 }
\xsp
          Type in {\sf  create.ibm}.  All files in 
the subdirectory {\it source} and {\it root} are copied in the 
current directory and you will get\\
\begin{indention}{12mm}%%
{\sf
               CFPTBL  ...   cfp table\\
               atgen   ...   csf generator\\
               atomci  ...   atomci program\\
               fpl     ...   FPL preprocessor\\
               fpl90lib.o  ...   FPL link library.\\

}
\end{indention}%%
          The three files, -- {\sf CFPTBL}, 
{\sf atgen} and {\sf atomci} -- are all you need
          for production run of ATOMCI. 
You should keep at least these three files.     
\xsp
          It also runs test jobs
\begin{indention}{12mm}%%
{\sf
                boron .stdin (Sample 1)\\
                f.atg and f.stdin (Sample 2)\\
                fpt.atg and fpt.stdin (Sample 3)\\
}
\end{indention}%%
          These jobs creates 
                {\sf boron.output f.NO etc}  in the main directory.
\xsp
          Sample outputs are stored in {\it output}.
          Timin data are also recorded in {\it output}.
\begin{indention}{12mm}%%
{\sf 
Tue May 31 12:10:48  1994\\
====================== atomci installation starts =====\\
Tue May 31 12:10:49  1994\\
======================================== make all =====\\
Tue May 31 12:10:50  1994\\
======================== atomci installation ends =====\\
Tue May 31 12:10:51  1994\\
================================= test run starts =====\\
Tue May 31 12:10:51  1994\\
=========================================== boron =====\\
Tue May 31 12:14:30  1994\\
=============================== fluorin mc and sd =====\\
Tue May 31 12:34:34  1994\\
====================================== fluorin pt =====\\
Tue May 31 12:37:43  1994\\
=================================== test run ends =====\\
}
\end{indention}%%
\xspace
{\bf Utilities}
\xsp
     THe {\sf atomdiff} script compares newly generated outputs to those in
     the subdirectory {\sf output}. 
To get the difference files,
type in.
\begin{indention}{12mm}%%
{\sf
       atomdiff boron\\
       atomdiff f\\
       atomdiff fpt\\ 
}
\end{indention}%%
\xsp
 In the  subdirectory {\sf root}, there are documentation for FPL, 
{\sf fpl90.doc}
and brief descriptions
about file management of ATOMCI systems,  {\sf README} file,
\xspace
\section*{SAMPLE 1: CI calculations on the ground state of Boron.}
\xspace
The {\bf boron.stdin}
file included in the package is a sample input file
for General CI on 
the ground \xsup{2}P\xsub{o} state of
the Boron atom. For sake of brevity we do not reproduce here the
whole input list as it appears in the actual file.
\xsp
{\bf  Run by typing }
\xsp
{\sf
atomci $<$ boron.stdin $>$ boron.output \&
}
\xsp
The results are in the directory {\it output}.
\xspace
\section*{SAMPLE 2: CI calculations on the ground state of Fluorine}
\xspace
The {\bf f.stdin}
file included in the package is a sample input file
which allows to perform a SD-CI calculation on the
ground \xsup{2}P\xsub{o} state of Fluorine using a set
of 35 Spherical GTO's (13s,11p,6d,5f) and neglecting single and
double excitations from the 1s core orbital. The natural orbitals are
determined and then used to repeat the calculation.
Notice the use of the \getnamelist NAMELIST to select the NO's
from the first calculation.
\xsp
The first step will be the generation of the {\sf f.csf} input.
\xspace
{\bf atgen $<$ f.atg }
\xsp
Proceed then to perform the actual CI calculation
\xspace
{\bf {\sf atomci $<$ f.stdin $>$ f.output \&} }
\xsp
The results are in the directory {\it output}.
\xspace
Notice that the WFMAIN and AIMAIN steps are not repeated in the second
run, since all the necessary files (EE, AO integrals) do not depend
on the actual orbitals, and thus do not need to be regenerated.
ITVCI=1 in the natural orbitals iteration. In this case the
transformation coefficients are taken from the second record on
unit 27 ({\sf f.NO}),
i.e. are the natural orbitals generated in the
first run (cfr. IOFLG=0,-1,-1,0,0, in both the first and second run).
Notice also that the first 16 elements of the IND array could be
reduced equivalently to 8, i.e.
\xsp
 IND= 2,0,1,2,  2,0,3,13,   2,1,1,1,  2,1,2,11, ... 
\xsp
is equivalent to
\xsp
 IND= 2,0,1,13, 2,1,1,11, ...
\xspace
\section*{SAMPLE 3: PT-CI calculations on the ground state of Fluorine}
\xspace
The last example is 
a perturbative selection CI calculation on the
ground \xsup{2}P\xsub{o} state of Fluorine using a set
of 35 Spherical GTO's (13s,11p,6d,5f) and neglecting single and
double excitations from the 1s core orbital
({\bf fpt.stdin}) . The natural orbitals are selected 
from the previous  calculation. 
\xsp
{\bf  Run by typing }
\xsp
{\sf
atgen  $<$ fpt.atg\\ 
atomci $<$ fpt.stdin $>$ fpt.output 
}
\xsp
The results are in the directory {\it output}.
